1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,569
It fits in the palm of your hand.

2
00:00:02,569 --> 00:00:03,856
It is made of gold.

3
00:00:03,856 --> 00:00:06,091
It is about a thousand years old.

4
00:00:06,091 --> 00:00:10,766
And millions of people have been told it is a model of a fighter jet.

5
00:00:11,233 --> 00:00:15,194
A jet, in the Colombian jungle, a thousand years ago.

6
00:00:15,194 --> 00:00:17,809
Delta wings, a cockpit, a tail fin.

7
00:00:17,809 --> 00:00:20,500
You have probably seen the pictures.

8
00:00:20,966 --> 00:00:23,531
So tonight we do something different.

9
00:00:23,531 --> 00:00:25,333
We treat it as a stranger.

10
00:00:25,333 --> 00:00:31,433
An unknown specimen, and we try to identify the species, using only what we can measure.

11
00:00:31,899 --> 00:00:32,980
Specimen sheet.

12
00:00:32,980 --> 00:00:34,348
Witness statements.

13
00:00:34,348 --> 00:00:35,212
Field marks.

14
00:00:35,212 --> 00:00:36,725
A look at its family.

15
00:00:36,725 --> 00:00:41,766
Then a replica that flew, and a laboratory test from this very decade.

16
00:00:42,233 --> 00:00:44,072
Every claim gets a score.

17
00:00:44,072 --> 00:00:46,279
At the end, an identification.

18
00:00:46,279 --> 00:00:47,750
Aircraft, or animal.

19
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:48,633
Let's begin.

20
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:54,217
Specimen sheet.

21
00:00:54,217 --> 00:01:02,933
Gold pendants of this shape are held by the Gold Museum in Bogotá, and by the Metropolitan Museum of Art in New York.

22
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:06,733
Most are five to seven and a half centimetres long.

23
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:12,871
They were cast in gold-copper alloy, called tumbaga, by the lost-wax method.

24
00:01:12,871 --> 00:01:18,467
Carve a model in wax, wrap it in clay, melt the wax out, pour in the metal.

25
00:01:18,467 --> 00:01:20,333
Fine work, at tiny scale.

26
00:01:20,799 --> 00:01:26,976
The Met files them under flying fish pendant, culture Tolima, with a question mark.

27
00:01:26,976 --> 00:01:30,995
The Tolima lived in the valley of the Magdalena River.

28
00:01:30,995 --> 00:01:34,418
The Quimbaya lived farther west, on the Cauca.

29
00:01:34,418 --> 00:01:36,799
The internet mixes up the names.

30
00:01:37,266 --> 00:01:38,813
And the dates wobble.

31
00:01:38,813 --> 00:01:44,706
Anywhere from five hundred B C to fifteen hundred A D, depending on who you ask.

32
00:01:44,706 --> 00:01:45,000
Why?

33
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,873
Because these pieces came from looters.

34
00:01:47,873 --> 00:01:52,366
Few were dug by archaeologists, so the grave context is gone.

35
00:01:52,833 --> 00:02:01,311
Wikipedia warns that a lot of so-called Quimbaya gold has been restored, reshaped, or copied for the collector market.

36
00:02:01,311 --> 00:02:05,766
So even before the first theory, our specimen has a thin file.

37
00:02:06,233 --> 00:02:10,455
Still, enough survive in enough museums to study the type.

38
00:02:10,455 --> 00:02:13,440
A real object, a fuzzy birth certificate.

39
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:15,333
Now, who first said plane?

40
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:17,020
Witness statements.

41
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:23,124
Ivan Sanderson was a Scottish-born naturalist who hunted for Bigfoot and the Loch Ness monster.

42
00:02:23,124 --> 00:02:29,099
He examined one of these pendants and said it looked too mechanical to be a natural creature.

43
00:02:29,566 --> 00:02:37,742
Another witness, an aviation expert named Ulrich, is quoted as saying it closely resembled a Swedish Saab jet.

44
00:02:37,742 --> 00:02:39,377
Notice what these are.

45
00:02:39,377 --> 00:02:40,269
Impressions.

46
00:02:40,269 --> 00:02:41,533
Not measurements.

47
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:43,505
Then came television.

48
00:02:43,505 --> 00:02:53,400
In two thousand nine, the series Ancient Aliens gave the pieces a catchier name, Tolima fighter jets, and a script that millions repeated.

49
00:02:53,866 --> 00:02:58,810
Its story: tomb robbers dug up a grave beside the Magdalena River.

50
00:02:58,810 --> 00:03:01,207
Hundreds of little gold figures.

51
00:03:01,207 --> 00:03:03,605
Most look like insects and fish.

52
00:03:03,605 --> 00:03:07,800
But about a dozen, it says, are eerily like modern jets.

53
00:03:08,266 --> 00:03:10,344
The key argument is one line.

54
00:03:10,344 --> 00:03:14,501
There is not a single insect with its wings at the bottom.

55
00:03:14,501 --> 00:03:17,726
So if it is not an insect, one thing remains.

56
00:03:17,726 --> 00:03:18,300
A plane.

57
00:03:18,766 --> 00:03:19,207
Pause.

58
00:03:19,207 --> 00:03:20,896
That is a false choice.

59
00:03:20,896 --> 00:03:25,376
It lists insect and aircraft, and leaves out everything else.

60
00:03:25,376 --> 00:03:25,743
Fish.

61
00:03:25,743 --> 00:03:26,184
Birds.

62
00:03:26,184 --> 00:03:26,624
Frogs.

63
00:03:26,624 --> 00:03:26,991
Bats.

64
00:03:26,991 --> 00:03:27,579
Lizards.

65
00:03:27,579 --> 00:03:32,500
Which, as we are about to see, is most of what the same hands made.

66
00:03:32,966 --> 00:03:35,457
And the numbers do not help the story.

67
00:03:35,457 --> 00:03:40,044
Even the believers' own count is about a dozen shapes out of hundreds.

68
00:03:40,044 --> 00:03:44,566
A culture that flew jets would surely have drawn more than a handful.

69
00:03:45,033 --> 00:03:49,900
So the witnesses give us a hunch, a catchy nickname, and a logical gap.

70
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:52,230
What does the specimen itself say?

71
00:03:52,230 --> 00:03:53,533
To the field marks.

72
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,321
Time to look closely, the way a naturalist would.

73
00:03:57,321 --> 00:03:58,066
Head first.

74
00:03:58,533 --> 00:03:59,608
Field mark one.

75
00:03:59,608 --> 00:04:00,253
The head.

76
00:04:00,253 --> 00:04:05,700
Round, domed, with two clear eyes and a short snout lined with little teeth.

77
00:04:05,700 --> 00:04:10,933
The Met catalogue notes these toothy snouts on every example in the type.

78
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:12,698
Is that a cockpit?

79
00:04:12,698 --> 00:04:16,522
A cockpit is a smooth bubble, with a pilot behind it.

80
00:04:16,522 --> 00:04:18,397
Here, the front is a face.

81
00:04:18,397 --> 00:04:19,696
Eyes, and a mouth.

82
00:04:19,696 --> 00:04:21,499
Planes do not have teeth.

83
00:04:21,966 --> 00:04:23,072
Field mark two.

84
00:04:23,072 --> 00:04:23,809
The wings.

85
00:04:23,809 --> 00:04:29,337
They carry a delicate grid pattern, and little spiral curls along the edge.

86
00:04:29,337 --> 00:04:32,138
Real jet wings carry control surfaces.

87
00:04:32,138 --> 00:04:33,833
These carry decoration.

88
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:35,577
Field mark three.

89
00:04:35,577 --> 00:04:36,253
The tail.

90
00:04:36,253 --> 00:04:39,709
One tall upright fin, set behind a round body.

91
00:04:39,709 --> 00:04:42,639
That is the feature everyone points at.

92
00:04:42,639 --> 00:04:44,292
A vertical stabiliser.

93
00:04:44,292 --> 00:04:48,799
And also, the tail of almost every fish that has ever lived.

94
00:04:49,266 --> 00:04:51,259
Look at a real river fish.

95
00:04:51,259 --> 00:04:53,023
A single vertical tail.

96
00:04:53,023 --> 00:04:55,706
Two side fins, held out like wings.

97
00:04:55,706 --> 00:04:57,163
And a rounded body.

98
00:04:57,163 --> 00:05:00,766
Take away the scales, and call that a fuselage.

99
00:05:01,233 --> 00:05:02,804
Some fish go further.

100
00:05:02,804 --> 00:05:07,368
Flying fish spread their side fins and glide through the air.

101
00:05:07,368 --> 00:05:12,006
The Met's catalogue suggests exactly that as a possible model.

102
00:05:12,006 --> 00:05:13,054
A flying fish.

103
00:05:13,054 --> 00:05:14,699
Hence the museum name.

104
00:05:15,166 --> 00:05:16,337
Field mark four.

105
00:05:16,337 --> 00:05:18,093
The way the maker draws.

106
00:05:18,093 --> 00:05:23,875
A frog on a similar pendant is flat and symmetrical, with its legs splayed out.

107
00:05:23,875 --> 00:05:25,631
No frog looks like that.

108
00:05:25,631 --> 00:05:27,899
Nobody says it is a spacecraft.

109
00:05:28,366 --> 00:05:28,835
A bat.

110
00:05:28,835 --> 00:05:29,381
A bird.

111
00:05:29,381 --> 00:05:30,319
A crocodile.

112
00:05:30,319 --> 00:05:33,990
All reduced to bold, sharp, symmetrical shapes.

113
00:05:33,990 --> 00:05:35,317
This was a style.

114
00:05:35,317 --> 00:05:39,066
Pendants were drawn like symbols, not portraits.

115
00:05:39,533 --> 00:05:41,415
One specimen is a mystery.

116
00:05:41,415 --> 00:05:44,890
A whole drawer of related specimens is a family.

117
00:05:44,890 --> 00:05:46,699
So let's meet the family.

118
00:05:47,166 --> 00:05:47,707
Lineup.

119
00:05:47,707 --> 00:05:55,204
In the Gold Museum in Bogotá there are over one hundred of these small animal figures on display.

120
00:05:55,204 --> 00:05:58,605
Birds, insects, fish, bats, frogs, reptiles.

121
00:05:58,605 --> 00:06:01,233
All from the same broad tradition.

122
00:06:01,699 --> 00:06:03,298
Here is the surprise.

123
00:06:03,298 --> 00:06:07,943
The Met describes a Tolima figure pendant of the winged type.

124
00:06:07,943 --> 00:06:12,740
A human body with splayed, fringed arms, and a big forked tail.

125
00:06:12,740 --> 00:06:13,882
The same hands.

126
00:06:13,882 --> 00:06:15,633
The same flat symmetry.

127
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:17,658
Set them side by side.

128
00:06:17,658 --> 00:06:19,004
The tail is shared.

129
00:06:19,004 --> 00:06:20,987
The sharp outline is shared.

130
00:06:20,987 --> 00:06:22,475
The wings are shared.

131
00:06:22,475 --> 00:06:26,866
If the fish is a plane, then the winged person is a plane too.

132
00:06:26,866 --> 00:06:28,566
That is a lot of planes.

133
00:06:29,033 --> 00:06:31,347
Debunkers add another candidate.

134
00:06:31,347 --> 00:06:40,823
The suckermouth catfish, a common river fish, with a rounded head, large eyes, stiff fins and small bumps on the front of its fins.

135
00:06:40,823 --> 00:06:43,933
Wikipedia lists it, along with flying fish.

136
00:06:44,399 --> 00:06:48,711
These were farmers, fishermen and goldsmiths of river valleys.

137
00:06:48,711 --> 00:06:51,214
Fish were food, and a thing to draw.

138
00:06:51,214 --> 00:06:56,500
Nothing in the finds suggests anyone there knew about taking off or landing.

139
00:06:56,966 --> 00:06:59,003
Skeptoid makes the same point.

140
00:06:59,003 --> 00:07:03,621
Fish have one vertical tail fin, and side fins that look like wings.

141
00:07:03,621 --> 00:07:09,733
The programme considered insects, and then jumped to aircraft, without ever checking fish.

142
00:07:10,199 --> 00:07:13,321
So on appearance alone, the type is an animal.

143
00:07:13,321 --> 00:07:17,324
But the believers have one more card, and it is a good one.

144
00:07:17,324 --> 00:07:19,699
They say: we built it, and it flew.

145
00:07:20,166 --> 00:07:20,847
Test one.

146
00:07:20,847 --> 00:07:21,756
The replica.

147
00:07:21,756 --> 00:07:33,866
In nineteen ninety-four, three Germans, Algund Eenboom, Peter Belting and Conrad Lubbers, built a radio-controlled model of one pendant, enlarged sixteen times.

148
00:07:34,333 --> 00:07:39,845
About a metre across, ninety centimetres long, seven hundred and fifty grams.

149
00:07:39,845 --> 00:07:41,850
They named it the Goldflyer.

150
00:07:41,850 --> 00:07:42,709
And it flew.

151
00:07:42,709 --> 00:07:46,933
Stable, controllable, filmed, and then shown on television.

152
00:07:47,399 --> 00:07:52,567
The claim that went with the film: they did not add an inch or remove an inch.

153
00:07:52,567 --> 00:07:57,866
Everything, they said, was done by the native people two thousand years earlier.

154
00:07:58,333 --> 00:07:59,966
Now read the build sheet.

155
00:07:59,966 --> 00:08:03,232
A propeller on the nose, and an engine to turn it.

156
00:08:03,232 --> 00:08:04,800
That is not in the gold.

157
00:08:05,266 --> 00:08:06,870
The gold wings are flat.

158
00:08:06,870 --> 00:08:11,884
The model's wings were curved on top, with movable flaps added at the back.

159
00:08:11,884 --> 00:08:17,699
And the big spirals along the front edge, which would wreck the airflow, were left off.

160
00:08:18,166 --> 00:08:19,607
Landing gear, added.

161
00:08:19,607 --> 00:08:21,121
Radio control, added.

162
00:08:21,121 --> 00:08:25,301
Motor power, trim and balance, all chosen by the builders.

163
00:08:25,301 --> 00:08:28,399
Not one of those is written in the pendant.

164
00:08:28,866 --> 00:08:35,556
With enough power and the right trim, almost any compact winged shape can be made to fly.

165
00:08:35,556 --> 00:08:36,383
Planks fly.

166
00:08:36,383 --> 00:08:37,586
Paper darts fly.

167
00:08:37,586 --> 00:08:41,044
A pendant, redesigned into an aircraft, flies.

168
00:08:41,044 --> 00:08:43,600
That tells us about the engineers.

169
00:08:44,066 --> 00:08:46,871
It proves that the shape is not absurd.

170
00:08:46,871 --> 00:08:48,741
And that is all it proves.

171
00:08:48,741 --> 00:08:54,566
Whether anyone in ancient Colombia ever wanted it to fly is a different question.

172
00:08:55,033 --> 00:08:59,729
For that question, we need a proper test, with no propeller and no engine.

173
00:08:59,729 --> 00:09:01,633
Just the pendant, and the air.

174
00:09:02,100 --> 00:09:02,800
Test two.

175
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:03,969
The laboratory.

176
00:09:03,969 --> 00:09:17,833
In twenty twenty-five, the aerospace engineer Sebastian Valencia and Jaime Orduy scanned a Tolima pendant in three dimensions and rebuilt it, exactly as it is, as a digital twin.

177
00:09:18,299 --> 00:09:29,899
They ran computer simulations, and then real wind tunnel tests, at wind speeds from two up to thirty metres a second, tilting the model from zero to fifteen degrees.

178
00:09:30,366 --> 00:09:32,638
The result surprised even them.

179
00:09:32,638 --> 00:09:42,899
A peak lift coefficient of one point eight seven, comparable to a modern delta wing, with stable vortices streaming along the leading edges.

180
00:09:43,366 --> 00:09:46,620
So the pendant really is a good lifting shape.

181
00:09:46,620 --> 00:09:52,703
Delta wings generate lift with swirling vortices, and this tiny gold fish does it too.

182
00:09:52,703 --> 00:09:54,966
Believers saw that, and cheered.

183
00:09:55,433 --> 00:09:58,884
But read the conclusion in the Journal of Aircraft.

184
00:09:58,884 --> 00:10:08,833
The authors say the flying qualities most likely arose as an incidental by-product of an aesthetic and ritual design, not from a pursuit of flight.

185
00:10:09,299 --> 00:10:10,797
Think of a maple seed.

186
00:10:10,797 --> 00:10:12,566
It spins down beautifully.

187
00:10:12,566 --> 00:10:15,424
Nobody carved the seed to be a helicopter.

188
00:10:15,424 --> 00:10:22,366
Shapes that please the eye, and shapes that move well in air, overlap more often than you would think.

189
00:10:22,833 --> 00:10:24,432
Two tests, one pattern.

190
00:10:24,432 --> 00:10:26,100
It could be made to fly.

191
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:28,463
It was never shown to be meant to.

192
00:10:28,463 --> 00:10:31,244
And that brings us to the last question.

193
00:10:31,244 --> 00:10:34,233
If not a plane, why a gold fish with wings?

194
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:35,438
Last item.

195
00:10:35,438 --> 00:10:36,028
Purpose.

196
00:10:36,028 --> 00:10:38,981
Gold in the ancient Andes was not money.

197
00:10:38,981 --> 00:10:44,666
It was a sacred metal, worn by leaders and priests, and buried with the dead.

198
00:10:45,133 --> 00:10:50,596
Many of these pendants were worn on cords around the neck, then placed in graves.

199
00:10:50,596 --> 00:10:56,733
One reading is that a creature moving between water and air suits a journey between worlds.

200
00:10:57,200 --> 00:11:01,584
The Met says Tolima pendants mix human and animal references.

201
00:11:01,584 --> 00:11:04,028
The exact meaning is not recorded.

202
00:11:04,028 --> 00:11:10,066
We do not know the maker's word for it, and anyone claiming to know is guessing too.

203
00:11:10,533 --> 00:11:12,415
Which is the honest ending.

204
00:11:12,415 --> 00:11:16,528
The best fit for the evidence is a stylised fish or animal.

205
00:11:16,528 --> 00:11:24,266
The aircraft idea needs a propeller and a tour guide, but it does not need anything from the Tolima themselves.

206
00:11:24,733 --> 00:11:26,273
And the alien version?

207
00:11:26,273 --> 00:11:30,543
It needs visitors who left no craft, no pilot, and no record.

208
00:11:30,543 --> 00:11:35,233
Just a dozen pieces of jewellery, in a tradition made of thousands.

209
00:11:35,700 --> 00:11:37,816
So here is the strange part.

210
00:11:37,816 --> 00:11:39,176
The shape is real.

211
00:11:39,176 --> 00:11:40,461
The lift is real.

212
00:11:40,461 --> 00:11:42,274
The coincidence is real.

213
00:11:42,274 --> 00:11:44,466
Only the aircraft is missing.

214
00:11:44,933 --> 00:11:46,069
Identification.

215
00:11:46,069 --> 00:11:48,266
Time to score the gold flyer.

216
00:11:48,733 --> 00:11:52,718
Real pre-Columbian gold, in major museums: evidence.

217
00:11:52,718 --> 00:11:56,856
Animal features, eyes, teeth, grid patterns: evidence.

218
00:11:56,856 --> 00:12:03,064
Shares its style with winged figures and animals in the same tradition: evidence.

219
00:12:03,064 --> 00:12:06,666
Best model, a flying fish or a catfish: likely.

220
00:12:07,133 --> 00:12:09,992
Built as an aircraft: no evidence.

221
00:12:09,992 --> 00:12:12,010
Flies as made: debunked.

222
00:12:12,010 --> 00:12:16,466
The 1994 model needed engine, flaps and landing gear.

223
00:12:16,466 --> 00:12:20,166
Visitors from elsewhere: no evidence at all.

224
00:12:20,633 --> 00:12:22,076
The identification.

225
00:12:22,076 --> 00:12:24,583
Stylised animal, probably a fish.

226
00:12:24,583 --> 00:12:25,798
Not an aircraft.

227
00:12:25,798 --> 00:12:33,166
It is a beautiful accident of design that flies well in a lab, and was never built to fly at all.

228
00:12:33,633 --> 00:12:41,862
Somewhere, a thousand years ago, a goldsmith shaped a little fish with wings, for someone who was travelling beyond this world.

229
00:12:41,862 --> 00:12:43,482
That is wonderful enough.

230
00:12:43,482 --> 00:12:45,166
It does not need a runway.

231
00:12:45,633 --> 00:12:53,785
Next case: nineteen sixty-seven, a calm night off Nova Scotia, and a glowing object that seemed to fall into the sea.

232
00:12:53,785 --> 00:12:55,666
Then the Navy went looking.

233
00:12:56,133 --> 00:12:57,758
What do you think they were?

234
00:12:57,758 --> 00:13:02,633
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
